The Gehennom Problem:
Why is Gehennom boring, and what should be done about it?

(Yes, this post is going to be rather long.  Sorry.  I hope it's
less boring than playing through Gehennom.)

Gehennom is boring for several key reasons...

 1. At this stage in the game, the player has few major goals left
    to fulfill, especially compared to the number of levels that
    remain to be traversed.  You've got some decent armor, bought
    the most critical first few increments of protection, identified
    most of the really critical potions and scrolls (and, in many
    cases, the important rings as well), wished for your favorite
    artifact if you wanted to, built your character up to a point
    where he can survive most things, and just generally done an
    awfully high percentage of the really interesting things.
    All you've really got left to do (in terms of major strategic
    goals) is deal with Vlad, clear the way down to the vibrating
    square, wake the wizard, get the amulet, and go ascend.  Three
    of these five goals are things you're not going to start until
    you get all the way to the bottom of Gehennom, so meanwhile
    you're really only working on two things:  deal with Vlad and
    clear the way to the vibrating square.  That's two things to
    do and about half the game to do them in.

 2. There are a lot of non-teleport levels, which makes trips back
    upstairs (e.g. to visit a stash) take longer than you'd like.
    This is exacerbated by the fact that several of these levels
    require you to follow circuitous paths each time you traverse
    them.  (Asmodeus' and Baalzebub's lairs are particularly
    egregious in this regard; Jubilex' is the major exception,
    having no walls.  O-town is also not too bad.)

 3. The levels that do allow teleport are mazes, which are very
    tedious to explore compared to most of the main dungeon.
    (They're also a pain to traverse subsequently if you have
    neither teleport control nor a pick-axe, but if you have either
    of those things they're MUCH less annoying than some of the
    demon lairs -- albeit still more trouble than most of the main
    Dugeons of Doom or arguably the Gnomish Mines.)

 4. Many of the enemies you encounter are an annoyance rather than
    a real threat.  Asmodeus is particularly galling, as all he can
    really do is pester you in an apparent attempt to make you *wish*
    you were dead.  The fact that he can teleport when you cannot
    just makes him that much more like an annoying playground brat,
    running up behind you, tapping you on the back, then running
    away yelling "Nya-nyanya-nya-nya you can't catch me!"

 5. There are few really interesting rewards.  By "rewards" here I
    don't mean things that improve the character's survival chances
    (by this stage in the game, you have most of that) but things
    that make the game more *interesting*.  The maze levels all
    start to look more or less the same after a while, and the
    demon lairs are identical in every single game, and most of
    the objects you're likely to encounter are either mundane or
    no longer really matter, since you've probably used the castle
    wand to fill any important missing slots in your kit, and food
    is no longer an issue unless you're on some kind of really
    unusual diet ("I only eat lichen corpses and candy bars").
    This is not to say that you're not collecting objects.  You
    may very well be collecting every potion you see, to use for
    alchemy.  But while these objects may be very useful when
    collected, they're not particularly interesting to find on
    an individual basis.  You do not, on the second level of
    Gehennom, typically find a yellow potion and wonder what new
    and exciting capabililty that might give you if you can ID it.

 6. There are few really interesting dangers, either (until you
    get to the Wizard, at which point things get dicey again).

So, to summarize:
 A. Not many major goals you can work on.
 B. Lots of levels that take a long time to traverse EVERY TIME.
 C. Lots of nearly-identical levels that take a long time to explore.
 D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
 E. Not enough intesting things to find.
 F. Not many interesting dangers to face.

There may be other reasons too, but those are the ones I've noticed.

Now, what can be done?  First, I want to talk about what's already
been tried.  I'm going to talk here about the Lethe Patch, then
the Heck Squared patch, and then SLASH'EM.  I haven't played all
of these variants, so other people are welcome to chip in, but
I thought it was important to familiarize myself in principle
with the approaches that have already been tried.

Lethe Patch:

1. Geryon, Dispater, Demogorgon, and Yeenoghu now have lairs.  Did
   I mention above that the preponderance of non-teleport demon lairs
   accounts for two of the five key things I've noticed that make
   Gehennom tedious?  How is adding more of them going to help?

2. Many levels contain amnesia-inducing water instead of the normal
   kind.  Yes, more amnesia will certainly make the game more fun.
   NetHack 4 didn't just change amnesia completely in an attempt
   to make it more scary and less annoying.  Oh wait, yes it did.
   What were the people behind the Lethe patch thinking here?

3. The Gulf of N'Kai certainly doesn't look boring...  unless you
   think five near-identical special levels in a row is boring,
   when there's nothing really unique or interesting about them.

4. The changes to the Sanctum don't really help anything, because
   the Sanctum isn't really one of the most boring aspects of the
   vanilla version of Gehennom.

5. The non-Gehennom changes that the patch makes likewise are
   unlikely to be a significant factor in making Gehennom less
   boring.

So as best I can tell from the info on the Wiki, nothing is really
done in the Lethe patch to address problems A, C, E, or F, and
meanwhile B and D are made actively worse.

On the whole, I'm not really inclined to bother playing the
Lethe Patch, because I can't see any way it could possibly be
an improvement.  If someone here is a big fan of it, please
feel free to explain what it is about this patch that makes
the game so much more interesting, because the description on
the wiki didn't really communicate that to me.

Heck Squared:

1. Geryon, Dispater, Demogorgon, and Yeenoghu now have lairs,
   but if I understand correctly you only encounter one of them,
   so that's an improvement over the Lethe patch.  Lumping in
   Asmodeus and Baalzebub into this group, so that you only
   get one of those six layers, would make it an improvement.

2. Some (all?) of the special levels in the heck squared patch
   come in multiple versions.  In vanilla, all the special levels
   above the Castle have at least two versions, and many of them
   have several, but from the castle down none of them do.  The
   heck squared patch corrects this omission, which IMO is very
   much a step in the right direction.  So that's good.

3. The maze levels can have various special things embedded in them.
   I like this idea in principle.  (Whether I will like the specifics
   is hard to say, as I haven't played with the patch yet.  I intend
   to at some point.)

So some attempt is made in the Heck Squared patch to address points
C and E, but nothing is really done, as far as I know, about poings
A, B, D, or F.  Better than nothing?

SLASH'EM's Approach to Gehennom:

1. Geryon, Dispater, Demogorgon, and Yeenoghu now have lairs.  Did
   I mention above that the preponderance of non-teleport demon lairs
   accounts for two of the five key things I've noticed that make
   Gehennom tedious?  How is adding more of them going to help?

2. On the other hand, Gehennom is now overall smaller, meaning there
   are fewer maze levels.  To compensate, there are more levels in
   the main dungeon, above the castle.  This probably does help make
   Gehennom less boring and tedious, but at the cost of making the
   game harder (particularly in the middle game).

3. One optional branch is added in Gehennom, but it's a small,
   single-level branch, so I'm not sure how much that really helps.

So SLASH'EM aggressively addresses problem C, but if it does much
for the other problems it's not clear from the info on the Wiki.
SLASH'EM is a variant I intend to play some day, but probably not
until I've ascended most of the roles in vanilla.

So, again, let's look at those problems...

 A. Not many major goals you can work on.
 B. Lots of levels that take a long time to traverse EVERY TIME.
 C. Lots of nearly-identical levels that take a long time to explore.
 D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
 E. Not enough intesting things to find.
 F. Not many interesting dangers to face.

SLASH'EM treats C ruthlessly, and Heck Squared addresses C and E.
If the Heck Squared approach to demon lairs were modified to lump
Baalzebub and Asmodeus (at least) in with the multiple-choice demon
lairs that you only see one of, that would help with B and maybe D.

None of the three sets of changes I've looked at appears to really
do much for A or F, unless I'm missing something.  So perhaps
those are the areas that someone should focus on, if someone were
to undertake to produce a patch to seriously improve Gehennom.

With that prelude, then, I'm going to start brainstorming.  I don't
suppose most of the ideas I'll come up with will be really practical,
but hey, maybe one of them will inspire someone to come up with a
better Gehennom improvement patch.

So, here we go...  Jonadab's Crazy Ideas to Improve Gehennom:

* Cherry-pick some of the better ideas from the patches and
  variants discussed above -- e.g., embed special rooms within
  some of the maze levels like Heck Squared does.

* Allow some levels in Gehennom to be room-and-corridor levels,
  similar to the ones in the main dungeon, but with different,
  more Gehennom-themed special rooms in them.

* Make demon lairs allow teleport after the associated demon is
  killed.  (I know, this is probably complicated to implement.)

* Introduce guaranteed levelport traps on some of the special
  levels (e.g., Orcus Town), so that players with teleport control
  can conveniently make a couple of stash trips without traversing
  every level twice per trip.

* Make certain objects very rare outside of Gehennom (or, if
  they are already rare, make them more common in Gehennom) so
  that the player will still be finding useful things they don't
  already have (or don't have in abundance).  Care would have to
  be taken here to preserve balance -- not to make the earlier
  game very much harder, for example -- while still providing
  loot that players can get somewhat excited about finding in
  Gehennom.  Off the top of my head, possible candidates for this
  treatment include horns of plenty (which are already not common
  elsewhere and would not be unbalancing if found in Gehennom,
  just cool), silver weapons (which are unlikely to be really
  needed much prior to Gehennom), boomerangs, scrolls of stinking
  cloud, and eggs that will hatch into pets if carried.  Also,
  making scrolls of magic mapping more common in Gehennom would
  probably not hurt anything much.

* Allow wands of digging to work in Gehennom the same way as in the
  rest of the dungeon, so that cutting paths is less tedious.
  (Although, this could also make the ascension run easier.)

* For standard maze levels, introduce probabilities that make the
  down stairs more likely to be somewhat near the up stairs on
  most levels, so that the average length of the path between them,
  once the levels are fully explored, is shorter in most games.
  Nothing drastic that would allow the player to locate the next
  stairs by just searching the immediate vicinity of the other ones;
  just tip the scales a little in favor of somewhat shorter paths
  between them on average.  Maybe something like, after initial
  placement, calculate the distance between the stairs and if
  rand(calculated_distance) > rand(5)*rand(6) then pick new random
  locations for the stairs.

* Allow *some* (not all) of the random maze levels to have
  two-tile-wide paths instead of one-tile-wide.

* Introduce interesting new enemies that are only generated late
  in the game.  I previously posted a Crazy Idea for "Yora Hawks".
  Okay, maybe they were a little over the top:  so tone them down.
  The R class could also stand to be expanded with at least a third
  kind of monster that does things to your inventory.  I posted an
  idea for that previously as well, which proved to be impractical
  in its particulars, but I still think the general idea of a third
  kind of R has merit.

* In Gehennom, it's always night.  Among other things, this would
  mean gremlins could always steal intrinsics in Gehennom.

* Introduce a handful of unique named player-character monsters,
  with special properties, who may appear in Gehennom.  I'm thinking
  at most one for each role, with the Wizard of Yendor having the
  wizard role covered already, and the High Priest of Moloch can
  probably be the priest one.  These would be always hostile...
   * So, for example, maybe anywhere below dungeon level 35 you
     may encounter Thargor, a particularly strong, very fast lawful
     human barbarian (yeah, I know, a player can't be lawful, but
     it fits better with the source material) who wields a dangerous
     (intelligent lawful barbarian) artifact broadsword named
     LifeReaper that takes away 20% of your remaining HP or 10% of
     your max HP per hit, whichever is more.  The sword should not
     be obtainable by wishing (and should become a mundane broadsword
     in bones), so the only way to get it is to take it from Thargor,
     who has several hundred hitpoints and reflection and player-
     style magic resistance.  If this seems insanely way too
     frighteningly overpowered, adjust the numbers downward until it
     only seems a little frighteningly overpowered (about as
     powerful as the Wizard of Yendor, but different, and without
     the ability to recover from death infinitely many times --
     though one recovery from death would fit well with the source).
     Only generated in Gehennom.  Being human, he ignores Elbereth.
   * For the ranger, I'm thinking a hobbit (but at much higher
     difficulty level than is normally possible for hobbits) named
     Belkar who dual-wields knives or daggers (I know players in the
     NetHack ranger role can't dual-wield; he's...  special) and
     speaks various trash-talk (sourced as direct quotes from OOTS
     strips where possible, e.g., he could remark on how your head
     will make a nice litter box for Mr. Scruffy).  Has a ring #named
     "Jumping +20" (of arbitrary type -- NetHack doesn't have a ring
     of jumping, and it's probably not worth adding one just so he
     can have it; just #name any ring).  Belkar should be very fast,
     able to jump, and have intrinsic stealth and be very deadly in
     melee, but he doesn't need any particularly strong combination
     of resistances.
   * Et cetera, for the other roles.

Yeah, I know, not all of these ideas are very good.  But maybe a
couple of them can serve as a starting point.  Will anyone argue
in all seriousness that Gehennom does not need any improvement?

One last time, the problems as I see them:

 A. Not many major goals you can work on.
 B. Lots of levels that take a long time to traverse EVERY TIME.
 C. Lots of nearly-identical levels that take a long time to explore.
 D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
 E. Not enough intesting things to find.
 F. Not many interesting dangers to face.


